 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d050: knot (k=8)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.1 and radius 1.76 with 8 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.37) and a 4-dim charge; 11 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 24 couplings (50% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 2 sweeps with slip rate 0.98 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 1.41 bonds per particle, largest molecule 5.0 particles, 10.0 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.107 vs ESN 1.000\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.104  random bonds 0.099\n  xor      0.507 vs ESN 0.665\u00b10.237  [match, at chance]  no bonds 0.520  random bonds 0.520\n  narma10  0.348 vs ESN 0.842\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.306  random bonds 0.272\n  latch    0.952 vs ESN 0.677\u00b10.009  [beat]  no bonds 0.948  random bonds 0.961\n  mnist14  0.553 vs ESN 0.839\u00b10.006  [below]  no bonds 0.423  random bonds 0.589\n\nGood at: latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
